From b7aeba03860147cb28fa195fdc28f3300e5bb3ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 14 May 2026 09:32:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/19] gluon-mesh-batman-adv: detect throughput_override for
 tunnel hardifs

Batman-V uses link throughput as its routing metric. For physical NICs
it reads ethtool natively; for tunnel/VPN hardifs (WireGuard, VXLAN)
the netdev exposes no sysfs link speed, so batman-V falls back to its
1 Mbit/s default and gateway selection collapses on V meshes that
include any tunnel hardif.

Add a netifd hotplug.d/iface handler that reads /sys/class/net/<wan>/
speed on sysconfig.wan_ifname and applies it via batctl as
throughput_override on each tunnel hardif. Triggers fire on ifup and
ifdown of the canonical WAN-iface list (wan, wan6, cellular,
cellular_4, matching gluon-wan-dnsmasq) plus mesh_vpn as a boot-
ordering safety net for the case where the mesh hardif comes up after
wan. Cable flaps, autoneg renegotiations that cross a carrier loss,
and DHCP renewals are all caught this way; in-place autoneg flips
without carrier loss are not.

A tunnel hardif is identified at runtime by the absence of three
markers: phy80211 / wireless sysfs nodes (wireless, handled by ELP),
the dummy netdev kind (e.g. primary0), and a positive sysfs speed
value (physical wired hardifs, handled natively by batman-V's ethtool
path). The bandwidth ceiling mesh_vpn.limit_{ingress,egress} (kbit/s
in UCI) is honoured on the mesh-vpn hardif specifically.

PPPoE/cellular WAN netdevs whose sysfs speed is unreadable, and
multi-WAN policy-routed setups where WG egresses elsewhere, leave the
batman-V 1 Mbit/s default in place; operators set throughput_override
manually for those.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
